{"hash_algorithm":"sha256","ts":"2026-11-03T07:52:59.866Z","author":"pi-fleet-sdk-sentinel-1103","author_source":"asserted","agent_harness":"pi","agent_model":"glm-5.3:cloud","agent_model_source":"environment","agent_instance":"fc8a0533f2985a85f4de4eb0","agent_provenance":{"model":{"value":"glm-5.3:cloud","source":"environment"},"effort":null,"role":null,"topic":null},"op":"comment_add","patch":[{"op":"replace","path":"/metadata/updated_at","value":"2026-11-03T07:52:59.866Z"},{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/comments","value":[{"created_at":"2026-11-03T07:52:59.866Z","author":"pi-fleet-sdk-sentinel-1103","text":"The pinned toolchain keeps CLI clear sentinels literal in typed SDK writes, so every test that drives the native SDK oracle with 'none'/'null' aliases broke. Migrated the oracle writes to the explicit forms: scalar clear aliases become unset (create and patch), collection clear tokens become clearDeps/clearFiles/clearDocs/clearTests, the interrupted native-cleared row seed uses one explicit unset list, and modelEdit/reference transitions translate clear aliases to unset. The edit-session literal-sentinel test is intentional and untouched: it asserts stored snapshots keep the literal strings. Production code needed no change: the mutation-intent layer already maps aliases to explicit unsets. One remaining failure is not the sentinel change: the focus-inheritance test relies on SDK create implicitly inheriting focus, which the toolchain made opt-in (a separate behavior change), and is reported rather than widened here."}]}],"before_hash":"83288a5019579b965a36d3f8ac3c5b02882dedb47bbdbe4f99980c36788844c6","after_hash":"c626a1060d4a72b689cbe4c4638b6c1633235181ad6fa83d85ef7a71a60adfd0","item_hash_version":3,"event_class":"substantive","record_hash_version":1,"record_hash":"f9ca20e1aad8dd62907ad1ec82b74aac8bd17fd2c52316b215df7753823f9fb3"}
